Priya Singh: Quick heads-up 1: our new Next SSR starter (next-ssr-eval) failed the parity run. Looks like differences in retry/auth + LangChain wiring. Repro steps below.

Priya Singh: Repro (local): clone examples/next-ssr-eval, pnpm install, set REDWOOD_API_KEY and REDWOOD_TEST_EXPIRE_MS=1200, run yarn dev, then curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/answer with sample prompt. JS path returns 401 and no retry; python sample retries and succeeds.

Jordan Lee: I can repro on macos CI runner. Log: "AuthError: token_expired -> 401 -> abort; no refresh queued". The thread in the eval harness marks it as regression for chat generation parity.

Marco Diaz: also noticed oddity in LangChain connector in the example: embedding call is followed immediately by a generate call and both share same opts.headers object. If JS SDK queues refresh and mutates headers, second call gets double-applied headers leading to 400 in some runtimes.

Elena Rosa: likely two issues: 1) auth interceptor needs proper queueing so inflight refresh blocks subsequent requests, 2) middleware must not mutate opts in-place. Suggested quick fixes: deep-clone opts before enqueuing and ensure auth refresh sets a single refreshed token and drains queue.

Priya Singh: Minimal repro snippet from pages/api/answer.js:```js
const emb = await redwood.embeddings.create({ input });
const out = await redwood.chat.create({ messages });
res.json(out);
```

Jordan Lee: temporary unblock for CI: set REDWOOD_SDK_ASSUME_RETRY=true in examples CI job so the client retries once on 401. Not ideal but gets parity green while we fix SDK.

Marco Diaz: another gotcha: LangChain example uses a custom callback that appends telemetry headers per-request; when the retry runs we end up with multiple x-rw-session headers concatenated which the backend rejects. We should normalize middleware to `set` not `append` and have SDK clone headers.

Elena Rosa: proposed SDK patch (pseudo):```js
// http/interceptor.js
if (resp.status === 401 && isSafeToRetry(opts)) {
  if (!auth.isRefreshing) {
    auth.isRefreshing = true;
    await auth.refreshToken();
    auth.isRefreshing = false;
    while (auth.queue.length) auth.queue.shift()();
  } else {
    return new Promise(res => auth.queue.push(() => res(http.request(cloneOpts(opts)))));
  }
}
```

Priya Singh: I'll open a PR in the example to avoid mutating headers in the LangChain callback and add a tiny test fixture that forces expiry then asserts a single trace header. Link in a moment.

Jordan Lee: also add an eval harness fixture that runs the SSR flow (server-render + api call) with forced expiry to catch regressions in CI.

devex-bot: CI ticket: https://ci.internal.redwood/gh/examples/next-ssr-eval/21 -> tests/eval/parity -> 2440012388

Marco Diaz: opening SDK PR with two changes: clone opts on enqueue and normalize headers to HeadersInit in middleware helpers. Will add unit tests covering queued refresh and header hygiene. PR WIP: https://github.com/redwood-inference/js-sdk/pull/223

Elena Rosa: I'll review and suggest adding isSafeToRetry guard that checks method + opts.idempotent to avoid retrying non-idempotent endpoints (important for LangChain mutate ops).

Priya Singh: PR in examples created to set REDWOOD_SDK_ASSUME_RETRY in CI, plus small callback fix in LangChain hook. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/examples/pull/145

Jordan Lee: once SDK PR lands, we'll remove the CI toggle and enable strict parity check again. Thanks team for quick turnaround.

Marco Diaz: local verification looks good after applying sdk clone + middleware change. Running full harness now.

Priya Singh: will follow up with a README note in the starter about how to simulate token expiry and opt-in flags for local dev. Closing loop when CI green.
